      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your suspicion of a core distribution error warrants solving the mean density formula, $\\rho_{mean} = \\frac{3M}{4\\pi R^3}$, using your specific mass and radius values to establish a baseline. If this calculated mean density is significantly lower than standard silicate or iron models, it implies an unrealistically large radius for that mass or an internal structure dominated by low-density material rather than a solid core. To resolve the discrepancy, you must verify whether the moon's equatorial and polar radii are being averaged correctly, as assuming a simplified spherical radius while observing an eccentric or oblate body will artificially dilute your density calculations. You can then model a potential interior profile with a central core and outer shell to determine if the current data mathematically permits a standard structure or truly suggests a hollow configuration.",
